Z||000011||lowCG||06-25-2000||01:33 AM||||I've got a set of those that came in my car,was sure glad to see them in place of the stock speakers when I popped a grill off to take a peek when I installed my new Pioneer 4 X 45 watt deck.Doesn't blow the windows out,but it sure sounds better than a stock setup.<BR>They'll probably work fine with your setup,and volume preference.||||reg||1
Z||000012||phillip||06-25-2000||09:49 AM||||i stuck my passive x-overs in the 4X10 spot so this is a guess. try tracing around one of your 4X10's onto a piece of  about 1/4" thick plexi, good plywood, or what ever you've got around to play with <BR>( you can do better if it works). find or borrow a couple of cheap 4's (even some blown out speaker from a flea market if you can get it cheap or free). that way you can size it all up with little cash invested. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Monkeyman||06-25-2000||10:02 AM||||Kenwood makes a nice set of 4x10s.  That's what I've got in the Grape Ape (at least until I get my separates).  Enormous improvement over stock.  About $100 give or take a little.||||reg||1
Z||000014||stillofknight||06-25-2000||11:19 PM||||Almost everyone still makes a 4x10.  GM is still using them in new cars, so the demand is still there.  All you have to do is look around.  Lanzar makes a decent set that can be had for about $60. ||||reg||1
Z||000015||tiacPaul||06-26-2000||01:25 AM||||I accidently bought those same 3 way Pyramid speakers (at the flea market) and my 40x4 watt sterio had them ratteling at low volume after about a month. <P>My new 2 way pioneers sound WAY better and have lasted much longer.  <P>How are you guys running so much power?  My dash rattles at 25 watts!||||reg||14
Z||000016||Tom88gt||06-28-2000||01:04 PM||||I got Mobile Authority 4x10 with poly drivers and dome tweeters.  $45 of a stereo web site.  I was going to go with JBLs but they were $80.  Pioneer/Jensen/Kenwoods are available from Crutchfield and some stores but are about $80+.<P>Nothing wrong with Pyramid or other cheap speakers.  Look at the back of most expensive speakers and famous brands..made in China/Taiwan/Malaysia/etc.  Its not where they are made but what they are made of and how they are made.  Look for poly, composite or treated drivers, not plain paper.  Dome tweeters, not cone tweeters.  Rubber surrounds, not foam.  Use of crossovers/capacitors.<P>I used to be a high end audio junkie.  Then I got a degree in marketing and leaned a lot of secrets.  Name brands are usually BS.  Read the label and do your research.  Know how to read and understand specs.  A lot of times, key elements are left out of the specifications printed on the package so the specs are meaningless.  It comes down to knowing what to look for.  For example, amplifier power means nothing without knowing at what voltage and THD level the measurment was taken.  <P>It's a global economy, and the name brands get their stuff built by the same factories that make the cheaper stuff.  Look past the name, the advertisment and the fancy packaging.  Read the specs and judge the product by what you can see, feel and hear.  Not what others tell you.<P>||||reg||14
Z||000017||Taijiguy||06-28-2000||02:32 PM||||I agree with you Tom. Specs don't really mean squat. One of the most important specs of an amplifier rating is commonly left out (slew rate) which makes all the difference in the world how accurate sound reproduction is. As for power, most times peak power rating is what's listed, and that's decieving. Many "name brand" items are actually manufactured by non name brand companies, and you end up paying for name only. When it comes to audio, I've always held fast to the rule that your ears have to be the final judge. Specs are designed for reference, not sound quality.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||Monkeyman||06-28-2000||05:47 PM||||Only slightly off subject...Whirlpool is famous for making a high quality product (washers, dryers, etc).  Roper is known to only a few (and sometimes referred to as the trailer brand).  They are made on the exact same assembly line, by the same workers, using the exact same components.  (No joke, guys!)  What's the difference?  Whirlpool is available in something like 17 different colors where as Roper is available in white.  And Whirlpool washers are available with the fancy digital display (guaranteed to break really soon and cost a bazillion bucks to fix) and Roper isn't.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Monkeyman||06-28-2000||05:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by doughboySE:<BR><B>the question of the day for me today is what you think? are these speakers ok? im going to go get a pair tomorrow to replace my stock front speekers   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> they are not performing well under the power of a 120 w amp at full power  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I LIKE IT LOUD  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>so what you think anyone have them? heard them?<BR>PLAY IT LOUD OR DONT PLAY IT AT ALL  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sometimes louder <I>isn't</I> better.  Mozart just doesn't sound good screaming in your ears.  (On the other hand, Dokken does! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)<P>||||reg||1
